1
00:00:00,754 --> 00:00:03,917
[Chattering]

2
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,795
- I gotta go.
- No, no. He'll be here.

3
00:00:13,869 --> 00:00:17,600
- I'm sure he's just running a little late.
- He's canceled two exams. He's not gonna-

4
00:00:17,672 --> 00:00:19,799
He's scared of you.
Sure.

5
00:00:19,875 --> 00:00:22,070
The ex-boy toy.
Yeah, that makes sense.

6
00:00:22,143 --> 00:00:24,373
- He wasn't scared before.
- Right.

7
00:00:24,446 --> 00:00:26,744
You think being afraid of me
is a symptom of a serious ailment?

8
00:00:26,815 --> 00:00:29,409
Sudden mood swings,
infantile regression, abdominal pain.

9
00:00:29,484 --> 00:00:32,385
He's passed out twice.
Yeah, I think it might be
a medical problem.

10
00:00:32,454 --> 00:00:35,048
- He's 20 minutes late.
I'm out of here.
- Please.

11
00:00:36,125 --> 00:00:38,320
He'll be here.
Why?

12
00:00:38,394 --> 00:00:40,555
Because he loves you
and does everything he's told?

13
00:00:40,630 --> 00:00:44,088
Because I didn't tell him you'd be here.

14
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:55,237
- He likes to see.
- Yes, he does.

15
00:00:56,513 --> 00:00:58,481
Stace?

16
00:00:59,383 --> 00:01:01,647
- What's going on?
- Hey.

17
00:01:01,718 --> 00:01:03,811
Hi. I'm Greg House.
You must be Matt.

18
00:01:03,887 --> 00:01:06,412
- Mark.
- Mark Warner. Don't get up.

19
00:01:07,357 --> 00:01:10,485
Sorry. Parent conferences.
Hi.
It's okay.

20
00:01:11,428 --> 00:01:14,659
- Wow. Greg House.
- Yeah. Wow.

21
00:01:14,731 --> 00:01:17,666
No, I haven't been avoiding you.
I just didn't want to waste your time.

22
00:01:17,735 --> 00:01:20,932
The other doctors checked me out.
They said it was just stress.

23
00:01:21,005 --> 00:01:24,463
- College season.
Kids, parents-They're all over me.
- Makes sense to me.

24
00:01:24,542 --> 00:01:26,840
- Greg.
- What do you want me to do?
- You said you'd check him out.

25
00:01:26,911 --> 00:01:29,209
He says he's healthy.
What's to check out?

26
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:33,182
Sorry for the mix-up,
but I'm glad you two
got a chance to catch up.

27
00:01:33,251 --> 00:01:36,118
- Looks like you're having fun.
- Oh, he's good.

28
00:01:36,955 --> 00:01:40,789
If you can fake sincerity,
you can fake pretty much anything.

29
00:01:40,859 --> 00:01:44,693
- I can't tell you how much I like your fella.
- Yeah, me too.

30
00:01:44,763 --> 00:01:46,856
You know,
I thought you'd be all sarcastic.

31
00:01:46,932 --> 00:01:51,392
Bitter, you know,
because Stacy married me.

32
00:01:51,470 --> 00:01:55,406
We should do things together.
Maybe throw a ball around
or something. Guy stuff.

33
00:01:55,474 --> 00:01:58,034
- We could go for a run together.
- Ah! It's Oscar Wilde.

34
00:01:58,110 --> 00:02:00,510
Wow. This pissing contest
is really turning me on.

35
00:02:00,579 --> 00:02:02,706
He needs to go to the hospital.
Here's to women.

36
00:02:02,782 --> 00:02:07,151
Can't live with 'em,
you can't kill 'em and tell the neighbors
they're stripping in Atlantic City.

37
00:02:07,220 --> 00:02:09,484
Damn straight.

38
00:02:14,394 --> 00:02:17,454
- I'm definitely taller.
- I have more hair.

39
00:02:17,530 --> 00:02:20,021
And I think that-Whoa.

40
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:23,069
- Mark?
- [Moaning]

41
00:02:24,472 --> 00:02:27,873
- Someone call 911 for a wagon
to Princeton Plainsboro?
- Garcon!

42
00:02:29,643 --> 00:02:33,204
- [Woman] What happened?
- It's okay, ladies and gentlemen.
Nothing to worry about.

43
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:35,578
- Unless you had the veal.
- You dosed him.

44
00:02:35,649 --> 00:02:39,346
I told you I'd check him out.
I was a little worried they were gonna
get here before he passed out.

45
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:42,218
Would have been tougher
to get him to drink.

46
00:02:42,289 --> 00:02:44,587
I'll give you a ride.
We can talk.

47
00:03:24,634 --> 00:03:27,330
[Cameron]
A tummy ache, cranky-
no apparent source.

48
00:03:28,772 --> 00:03:31,639
Any thoughts?

49
00:03:31,708 --> 00:03:35,166
Foreman, you're gonna contribute,
or are you too tired from stealing cars?

50
00:03:39,149 --> 00:03:42,846
- I'm being House. It's funny.
- I know. You made milk come out of my nose.

51
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:46,913
Morning. You guys got the file.
What's wrong?

52
00:03:46,990 --> 00:03:50,426
Previous tests revealed nothing
that would cause abdominal pain
or the mood swings.

53
00:03:50,495 --> 00:03:54,124
Then we're done.
What do you think? Ball game? Zoo?

54
00:03:54,199 --> 00:03:56,690
- I don't care.
I just want to hang with you guys.
- What about drugs?

55
00:03:56,768 --> 00:04:00,295
His tox screen on admission showed
a massive amount of chloral hydrate.

56
00:04:00,371 --> 00:04:02,703
Yeah. Sorry. That was me.

57
00:04:02,774 --> 00:04:05,140
I had to dope him up
to get him in here.

58
00:04:05,210 --> 00:04:08,008
Guy doesn't think he's sick.
Who does?

59
00:04:08,079 --> 00:04:11,378
His wife.
The woman you used to live with.

60
00:04:11,449 --> 00:04:14,509
That's her Indian name.
On her driver's license it's Stacy.

61
00:04:14,587 --> 00:04:16,521
I assume you have a point.

62
00:04:16,589 --> 00:04:19,558
You believe her over the patient himself.
That's why we're taking this case.

63
00:04:19,625 --> 00:04:23,493
The truth?
I hear voices all the time
telling me to do stuff.

64
00:04:23,562 --> 00:04:26,224
- It's crazy, huh?
- What happened to "everybody lies"?

65
00:04:27,933 --> 00:04:30,527
I was lying.

66
00:04:30,603 --> 00:04:33,128
Do the things- the blah, blah, blah, blah.
All that stuff the other docs did.

67
00:04:33,205 --> 00:04:35,366
If that's negative,
ultrasound his belly.

68
00:04:35,442 --> 00:04:39,674
If that's negative, C.T. His abdomen
and pelvis with and without contrast.

69
00:04:39,746 --> 00:04:41,805
- Did I miss anything?
- Kitchen sink.

70
00:04:41,882 --> 00:04:45,477
Well, we could certainly give that-
Oh, you minx!

71
00:04:46,786 --> 00:04:51,723
What you're thinking is,
you're going to save him,
be a hero and win her back.

72
00:04:51,791 --> 00:04:54,259
It's always impressive,
that level of twisted narcissism.

73
00:04:54,327 --> 00:04:57,524
She's married.
Big clue I lost the game.

74
00:04:57,598 --> 00:05:01,159
You can't be within 50 feet
of Stacy Warner.

75
00:05:01,235 --> 00:05:03,169
Thought she wanted me
to treat him.

76
00:05:03,237 --> 00:05:05,228
Treat the husband,
stay away from the wife.

77
00:05:05,306 --> 00:05:08,002
But what if they get close to each other?
What do I do then?

78
00:05:08,075 --> 00:05:11,010
Hey. You have to treat this
like a regular case.

79
00:05:11,078 --> 00:05:14,844
Be yourself-
cold, uncaring, distant.

80
00:05:14,916 --> 00:05:18,044
Please, don't put me on a pedestal.

81
00:05:19,087 --> 00:05:24,889
We C.T.'d your abdomen.
Nothing that would explain
the stomach pain.

82
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:27,224
- [Sighs] What's the next move?
- Leaving.

83
00:05:27,296 --> 00:05:29,287
How many more tests do I need?

84
00:05:29,364 --> 00:05:32,197
How many more doctors need to clear me
before we can get back to our life?

85
00:05:32,267 --> 00:05:34,758
- Just one.
- House.

86
00:05:34,836 --> 00:05:38,203
It must be awkward
being treated by a man who used to
be involved with your wife.

87
00:05:42,111 --> 00:05:45,137
It's awkward being in a hospital
when there's nothing wrong with me.

88
00:05:47,817 --> 00:05:50,911
M.R.A.'s were clean,
which means he's probably fine.

89
00:05:50,987 --> 00:05:53,285
He doesn't seem paranoid.
He shows no signs of-

90
00:05:53,356 --> 00:05:55,881
No. It means we have no idea
what's wrong with him.

91
00:05:55,959 --> 00:05:58,655
Ben Goldstein says his schedule's locked.
He can't do it before tomorrow.

92
00:05:58,728 --> 00:06:01,128
No. Today. Call him,
tell him I'll make it work.

93
00:06:01,198 --> 00:06:03,291
[Scoffs]
You're cutting him open?

94
00:06:03,367 --> 00:06:06,564
Oh, hold it. There's no need
for exploratory surgery.

95
00:06:06,637 --> 00:06:08,502
Dr. Cameron has a diagnosis.

96
00:06:08,572 --> 00:06:11,769
No, I just think it's premature
and maybe irresponsible...

97
00:06:11,842 --> 00:06:14,106
to do exploratory surgery
before we know he's actually sick.

98
00:06:14,178 --> 00:06:17,113
No, it's "premature" to put him
on a list for hospice care.

99
00:06:17,181 --> 00:06:20,947
And it's "maybe irresponsible"
to imply my actions are not
rationally supported.

100
00:06:21,018 --> 00:06:23,282
All we have is his wife-
Who says his stomach hurts.

101
00:06:23,355 --> 00:06:26,586
Works for me.
The patient doesn't even think he's sick.
Why would he consent to-

102
00:06:26,658 --> 00:06:29,456
His wife's a lawyer.
She's very convincing.

103
00:06:29,527 --> 00:06:32,519
Call Goldstein. Surgery is on.

104
00:06:38,236 --> 00:06:40,898
[Monitor Beeping]

105
00:06:45,444 --> 00:06:47,935
Retract.

106
00:06:49,482 --> 00:06:51,416
Insert.

107
00:06:52,652 --> 00:06:55,553
Leslie versus Leslie
seems to be right on point,

108
00:06:55,621 --> 00:06:58,522
but I'm sure they're going to
try to distinguish it by-

109
00:06:58,591 --> 00:07:02,823
Sorry. They'll try to distinguish it
relying on the minority opinion.

110
00:07:05,431 --> 00:07:07,365
Double milk, no sugar.

111
00:07:07,434 --> 00:07:09,334
I like sugar now.

112
00:07:23,717 --> 00:07:26,652
Some people would be
annoyed by that.

113
00:07:29,557 --> 00:07:33,288
You know why people
sit in waiting rooms?
This is gonna be good.

114
00:07:33,361 --> 00:07:38,196
People think the closer they're
sitting to the operating room,
the more they care.

115
00:07:38,266 --> 00:07:41,827
That's why I'm here.
I'm not moving until everybody sees me.

116
00:07:46,474 --> 00:07:49,102
Are you doing anybody
besides Mark?

117
00:07:50,144 --> 00:07:54,410
It's a medical question.
Because if I am,
his paranoia isn't paranoia.

118
00:07:54,483 --> 00:07:57,680
It's a justified response
and therefore not a legitimate symptom.

119
00:07:57,753 --> 00:07:59,846
Knew you'd understand.

120
00:07:59,922 --> 00:08:03,551
On the other hand,
if it was really just a medical question,
you would have sent one of your people.

121
00:08:03,625 --> 00:08:05,684
Why just push my buttons
when you can push theirs too?

122
00:08:05,761 --> 00:08:10,198
Hey, Dr. Mandingo, go ask the wife
if she's been messin' around.

123
00:08:10,265 --> 00:08:14,827
But you're asking
because if I am... unfaithful,

124
00:08:14,904 --> 00:08:16,895
I might sleep with you.

125
00:08:18,808 --> 00:08:23,871
The answer's no.
I don't sleep around.
Make sure you note that in his file.

126
00:08:23,947 --> 00:08:25,881
Mrs. Warner.

127
00:08:25,949 --> 00:08:29,441
The surgery went well.
He's in Recovery.
You can see him now.

128
00:08:35,292 --> 00:08:37,988
Goldstein found nothing
but a distended bladder.

129
00:08:39,964 --> 00:08:42,057
Neurogenic bladder
isn't causing the pain.

130
00:08:42,133 --> 00:08:44,966
It also doesn't cause
personality changes.

131
00:08:45,035 --> 00:08:48,903
On the other hand,
it would completely account
for Cameron's diagnosis.

132
00:08:48,973 --> 00:08:51,737
The patient's completely healthy.

133
00:08:53,911 --> 00:08:56,243
Get me the video of the surgery.

134
00:10:02,784 --> 00:10:04,752
[Beeping]

135
00:10:07,188 --> 00:10:10,919
Dr. Mandingo, you're needed
at the plantation house.

136
00:10:15,296 --> 00:10:18,823
Well, don't everybody talk at once.

137
00:10:18,900 --> 00:10:21,368
There's nothing there.

138
00:10:25,074 --> 00:10:28,874
Stop looking at
the suspiciously empty bottle
and look at the screen.

139
00:10:28,944 --> 00:10:31,412
Here's why I get the big bucks.

140
00:10:31,480 --> 00:10:33,539
This is nothing.

141
00:10:33,616 --> 00:10:36,244
An enhanced version of nothing.

142
00:10:36,318 --> 00:10:40,550
This... is the problem.

143
00:10:45,195 --> 00:10:47,755
- Unbelievable.
- Tremors in the muscle fiber.

144
00:10:47,831 --> 00:10:49,765
That's not peristalsis.

145
00:10:50,934 --> 00:10:54,097
- That's abdominal epilepsy.
- Means there's some sort
of neurological problem.

146
00:10:55,805 --> 00:10:58,797
A time bomb in his brain.

147
00:10:59,976 --> 00:11:01,967
I forget.
Who said it was nothing?

148
00:11:08,586 --> 00:11:13,114
I saw a very small, diffuse
abnormality in the brain waves.
Probably white matter.

149
00:11:13,190 --> 00:11:15,181
Means his axonal nerves
are dying.

150
00:11:15,259 --> 00:11:19,320
Explains
the neurogenic bladder.
Enough nerves die, he dies.

151
00:11:19,397 --> 00:11:21,524
Global axonal nerve death.

152
00:11:21,599 --> 00:11:23,658
Likely causes are encephalitis
or Alzheimer's.

153
00:11:23,734 --> 00:11:25,998
Early onset Alzheimer's.
The worst.

154
00:11:26,070 --> 00:11:28,300
He won't die right away.
He'll just want to.

155
00:11:28,372 --> 00:11:31,398
I'll check the blood
for Alzheimer's protein markers.

156
00:11:31,476 --> 00:11:35,378
Last I heard, Alzheimer's
had a genetic component.
Did the patient have parents?

157
00:11:35,447 --> 00:11:38,007
Parents died in a car crash.
No history of dementia.

158
00:11:38,083 --> 00:11:41,951
Send C.S.F. For C.B.C.
And viral serologies
to rule out encephalitis...

159
00:11:42,020 --> 00:11:44,352
and get tau proteins
to check for Alzheimer's.

160
00:11:44,423 --> 00:11:46,948
And this still feels a little light.

161
00:11:47,025 --> 00:11:51,519
I took a complete medical history.
Check out their house.
Take Sparky with you.

162
00:11:51,598 --> 00:11:54,999
- They live in Short Hills. Two hours away.
- You can expense the tolls.

163
00:11:55,068 --> 00:11:57,298
You're not interested
in a medical history.

164
00:11:57,370 --> 00:11:59,861
You're a Peeping Tom
trying to spy on your ex.

165
00:11:59,939 --> 00:12:01,873
Her secret diary-
That's the main thing.

166
00:12:01,941 --> 00:12:05,377
But as long as you're there,
take a peek in the medicine cabinet.

167
00:12:05,445 --> 00:12:10,041
Check for toxins, heavy metals-
anything that might explain this
other than encephalitis or Alzheimer's.

168
00:12:12,553 --> 00:12:15,283
And get receipts for the tolls.

169
00:12:27,334 --> 00:12:30,167
Making lunch?
I assume that's for Mark.

170
00:12:31,238 --> 00:12:33,832
We know about his parents.
What about further back?

171
00:12:33,907 --> 00:12:37,274
Grandparents, uncles, aunts.
How was their health?
Greg hates fishing.

172
00:12:37,345 --> 00:12:40,246
He's got a theory.

173
00:12:40,315 --> 00:12:42,579
Most likely candidate right now
is Alzheimer's.

174
00:12:49,991 --> 00:12:53,483
No. There's been no memory loss.

175
00:12:53,561 --> 00:12:56,359
I mean, he forgets
where he left his keys,
but who doesn't?

176
00:12:56,432 --> 00:12:58,491
Any family history?

177
00:12:58,567 --> 00:13:00,831
Of... whacked-outness?

178
00:13:00,903 --> 00:13:04,862
His sister voted for Nader, twice.
That's about it.

179
00:13:07,410 --> 00:13:12,347
You were with House
when it happened- to his leg?

180
00:13:12,415 --> 00:13:14,349
Oh.

181
00:13:15,885 --> 00:13:18,046
You're interested in him.

182
00:13:18,121 --> 00:13:20,146
We went on one date.

183
00:13:20,223 --> 00:13:22,282
It didn't go very well.

184
00:13:22,359 --> 00:13:26,455
Our first date didn't either.
I was never gonna see him again.
A week later, I moved in.

185
00:13:26,530 --> 00:13:30,057
Five years.
What would you like to know?

186
00:13:32,302 --> 00:13:34,827
What was he like before his leg?

187
00:13:35,972 --> 00:13:37,906
Pretty much the same.

188
00:13:37,974 --> 00:13:40,807
[Beeping]

189
00:13:47,785 --> 00:13:50,879
He's clear.
No Alzheimer's.

190
00:13:50,955 --> 00:13:53,446
Yeah, that's what I figured.

191
00:14:02,568 --> 00:14:07,335
Serious mountain bike.
Hasn't been used in a while though.

192
00:14:07,406 --> 00:14:09,897
He switched to yoga.
Brand-new yoga mat and tape.

193
00:14:09,975 --> 00:14:12,000
Man's getting older.
Or it might indicate back pain.

194
00:14:12,077 --> 00:14:15,410
Wife would have mentioned it.
Yoga's good for picking up
the ladies too.

195
00:14:15,481 --> 00:14:17,972
Not when you do it
in your own home.

196
00:14:18,050 --> 00:14:21,781
Change could be just a change,
not a symptom.

197
00:14:23,021 --> 00:14:25,683
Chase.

198
00:14:26,826 --> 00:14:30,455
"Dear 'House' boys, a snack
for your highly illegal search.

199
00:14:30,530 --> 00:14:33,090
Hope you like oatmeal raisin.
Love, Stacy."

200
00:14:33,166 --> 00:14:36,932
Whoa. In a desk drawer,
hidden in the back
under some papers.

201
00:14:37,003 --> 00:14:38,937
Secret stash.

202
00:14:39,005 --> 00:14:43,408
Amphetamines.
Regular use could lead
to neurotoxicity.

203
00:14:43,476 --> 00:14:47,139
Explains the axonal nerve damage
and the personality issues.

204
00:14:47,214 --> 00:14:49,944
On the other hand,
"Prescribed to W. Brown."

205
00:14:50,017 --> 00:14:52,042
Fake name.
Fake prescription.

206
00:14:52,119 --> 00:14:55,179
Could be, but the prescribing doc,
his name's real.

207
00:14:55,256 --> 00:14:59,386
This guy's just had his license pulled
for writing illegal prescriptions
to high school kids.

208
00:14:59,460 --> 00:15:01,451
Mark's a high school
guidance counselor.

209
00:15:01,529 --> 00:15:04,362
And Mr. Brown's birth date
makes him 17 years old.

210
00:15:04,431 --> 00:15:08,595
Do you think maybe these
were confiscated by a high school
guidance counselor?

211
00:15:09,604 --> 00:15:13,040
Anything else?
Um, yeah, he switched
from mountain biking to yoga.

212
00:15:13,108 --> 00:15:15,042
- Could indicate-
- He's getting older.

213
00:15:15,110 --> 00:15:19,137
- What did the C.S.F. Say about encephalitis?
- Said no. Champagne tap.

214
00:15:19,214 --> 00:15:22,650
No red cells, no white cells.
Serology's negative.
Which means we're back to Alzheimer's.

215
00:15:22,717 --> 00:15:25,481
I told her he didn't have it.
The marker tests were negative.

216
00:15:25,554 --> 00:15:28,182
Well, then you
should have told her that.
He could still have it.

217
00:15:28,256 --> 00:15:31,748
PET scan will reveal
any changing metabolic activity
in the cerebral cortex.

218
00:15:31,828 --> 00:15:34,661
Run him through
and check his memory.

219
00:15:37,567 --> 00:15:40,764
We're going to
inject a chemical marker
called F.D.D.N.P.

220
00:15:40,837 --> 00:15:43,533
- Then I'm gonna ask you a series of questions.
- Test my memory.

221
00:15:43,606 --> 00:15:46,598
Yeah, but first we're gonna map out
some specific brain functions.

222
00:15:46,676 --> 00:15:49,702
You know, check out the engine
before we take the car for a drive.

223
00:15:56,253 --> 00:15:58,448
Checking up on me?

224
00:15:58,522 --> 00:16:01,389
- I like all the pretty lights.
- Yeah.

225
00:16:14,672 --> 00:16:17,140
Okay.
[On Speaker]
Here we go.

226
00:16:17,208 --> 00:16:19,733
Your full name?
Mark Warner.

227
00:16:21,012 --> 00:16:23,640
- Is your mother living?
- No.

228
00:16:23,715 --> 00:16:26,081
Limbic system's intact.

229
00:16:26,150 --> 00:16:29,278
Okay, say you find
a stamped envelope on the street.
What do you do?

230
00:16:29,354 --> 00:16:32,812
- Find a mailbox and mail it.
- Geez. What a guy.

231
00:16:34,092 --> 00:16:36,424
His frontal lobe is working
way better than mine.

232
00:16:37,496 --> 00:16:39,487
Do you remember
when you got married?

233
00:16:39,565 --> 00:16:41,863
Three years ago this July.
Who is that?

234
00:16:41,934 --> 00:16:44,767
What? Could be a problem
with his long-term memory.

235
00:16:44,837 --> 00:16:47,169
- Big church wedding?
- Is that House?

236
00:16:47,239 --> 00:16:50,970
He remembers voices.
This serves
no diagnostic purpose.

237
00:16:51,043 --> 00:16:53,534
I thought you skimped
on the limbic system there.

238
00:16:53,612 --> 00:16:55,978
Emotional reactions.
I just want to be thorough.

239
00:16:56,048 --> 00:17:00,144
Did the atheistic bride
wear a pretty white dress?

240
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:03,087
Was she thinking of you?
Is that what you medically
need to know?

241
00:17:03,156 --> 00:17:05,750
What jewelry did your bride wear?

242
00:17:05,825 --> 00:17:09,454
She never wears any jewelry,
except the cross her mother gave to her.

243
00:17:09,529 --> 00:17:12,293
No underwear either,
at least not that day.

244
00:17:12,365 --> 00:17:15,425
I remember 'cause she ripped
her pretty white dress off in the car.

245
00:17:15,502 --> 00:17:17,697
That the sort of answer
you're looking for, Doctor?

246
00:17:17,771 --> 00:17:20,672
I think I upset him.
You gotta stop this now.

247
00:17:20,741 --> 00:17:26,179
I remember the honeymoon was in Paris.
I remember 'cause we didn't leave
the room for two weeks.

248
00:17:26,247 --> 00:17:29,182
- You want the details on that?
- A little defensive.

249
00:17:29,250 --> 00:17:32,845
It's not paranoia
if someone's out to get you.

250
00:17:47,436 --> 00:17:50,132
Here we go.

251
00:17:51,673 --> 00:17:54,403
He's sick, paranoid, and you
keep hammering him about me?

252
00:17:54,476 --> 00:17:58,378
The questions were designed
to define the operational parameters
of his limbic system.

253
00:17:58,447 --> 00:18:02,315
Elevate the words all you want.
You were just screwing with him.
Low, even by your standards.

254
00:18:02,385 --> 00:18:04,580
Medical screwing. It's what I do.

255
00:18:04,654 --> 00:18:08,249
Then you run away like a 12-year-old.
Go hide on the roof like you always do.

256
00:18:08,324 --> 00:18:10,554
I haven't been up here in five years.

257
00:18:25,209 --> 00:18:27,769
I don't know
what's wrong with him.

258
00:18:29,646 --> 00:18:33,810
It's not Alzheimer's.
It's not encephalitis.

259
00:18:33,884 --> 00:18:36,352
It's not environmental.
It's not immunological.

260
00:18:36,420 --> 00:18:39,082
Every test is negative
every time.

261
00:18:39,156 --> 00:18:41,886
He's perfectly healthy,

262
00:18:41,959 --> 00:18:44,757
but his brain is dying.

263
00:18:51,403 --> 00:18:54,304
It never occurred to me...

264
00:18:54,372 --> 00:18:57,535
that you couldn't figure out
what's wrong.

265
00:19:14,527 --> 00:19:16,518
I haven't given up.

266
00:19:24,070 --> 00:19:26,664
So what do we do?

267
00:19:32,012 --> 00:19:33,946
We wait.

268
00:19:37,184 --> 00:19:39,652
For what?

269
00:19:39,719 --> 00:19:41,710
Something to change.

270
00:19:43,790 --> 00:19:47,453
It's one of the great tragedies of life.
Something always-

271
00:19:48,995 --> 00:19:50,986
Something always changes.

272
00:19:53,601 --> 00:19:56,092
[Beeping]

273
00:20:01,342 --> 00:20:03,276
Nurse?

274
00:20:04,278 --> 00:20:06,269
Nurse!

275
00:20:12,855 --> 00:20:15,153
What happened?
What's wrong?

276
00:20:15,224 --> 00:20:19,684
My toes-They were numb,
tingling, then nothing.

277
00:20:19,761 --> 00:20:21,524
No pain. Nothing.

278
00:20:21,597 --> 00:20:23,656
It's okay. They're going
to take care of you.

279
00:20:23,732 --> 00:20:25,893
I'm scared, Stacy.
Hold my hand.

280
00:20:29,838 --> 00:20:31,772
What?

281
00:20:40,650 --> 00:20:42,880
What's happening?

282
00:20:42,952 --> 00:20:46,854
Time marches on.
He's paralyzed.

283
00:20:57,902 --> 00:21:01,201
His symptoms mimic
a peripheral nervous system
under attack.

284
00:21:01,272 --> 00:21:05,572
But he's experiencing
significant paresthesias,
and he can't move his hands or toes.

285
00:21:05,643 --> 00:21:07,577
It's peripheral.

286
00:21:07,645 --> 00:21:10,239
Guillain-Barre syndrome
attacks there, not the brain.

287
00:21:10,314 --> 00:21:13,044
No. No. I already did
an indirect Coombs' test.

288
00:21:13,117 --> 00:21:15,108
No agglutination, no antibodies.

289
00:21:15,186 --> 00:21:17,051
Initiative. Like that.

290
00:21:17,121 --> 00:21:19,180
Start him on I.V. Immuno-

291
00:21:19,257 --> 00:21:22,658
No antibodies means
he doesn't have Guillain-Barre, period.

292
00:21:22,727 --> 00:21:25,025
Period?
More like dot, dot, dot.

293
00:21:25,096 --> 00:21:27,587
What ifhe has the virus
but isn't producing
those antibodies?

294
00:21:27,666 --> 00:21:32,035
Come on. The chances of that-
Didn't ask about the Vegas line.
I said "what if."

295
00:21:32,103 --> 00:21:35,504
It would mean he's sick,
and his body's not doing
anything about it.

296
00:21:35,574 --> 00:21:38,509
So we either fight it for him
or it's fatal.

297
00:21:38,577 --> 00:21:40,568
Fatal sounds very bad to me.

298
00:21:40,646 --> 00:21:44,138
Well, without the antibodies,
we can't even test for it.
Don't know if we're right.

299
00:21:46,118 --> 00:21:49,576
Treatment isn't all that dangerous.
Plasmapheresis and I.V.I.G.

300
00:21:49,655 --> 00:21:55,457
If it works, we're right.
If he dies... it was something else.

301
00:22:00,566 --> 00:22:02,557
So the paralysis
might not be permanent.

302
00:22:02,636 --> 00:22:05,764
That's our hope,
but the brain's tricky.
You never know.

303
00:22:09,643 --> 00:22:11,508
What was that?
What?

304
00:22:11,578 --> 00:22:15,514
- With the head. The look.
- He just wants to talk to me.

305
00:22:15,582 --> 00:22:19,643
- Well, if it was medical,
he should be talking to me.
- I'll be just outside the door.

306
00:22:19,720 --> 00:22:21,950
Leave! Go talk to him.

307
00:22:22,022 --> 00:22:26,356
- You're going to leave me anyway.
- No. That's not gonna happen.

308
00:22:26,427 --> 00:22:30,363
You left him, and he had a limp.
If I can't walk or hold you?

309
00:22:30,431 --> 00:22:33,457
Honey, I'm not going anywhere.
I'm not going to talk to him.

310
00:22:33,534 --> 00:22:37,334
- If I can't feed myself?
- Mark, what you're feeling-
It's not real. It's the virus.

311
00:22:38,706 --> 00:22:41,869
- Mark? What is it?
- Mark?

312
00:22:41,943 --> 00:22:46,175
His throat's closing up.
He can't breathe. Code blue! Hurry!

313
00:22:46,248 --> 00:22:49,046
He's having a reaction to the I.V.I.G.
I need epi, stat.

314
00:22:49,118 --> 00:22:51,143
[Gasping]

315
00:22:51,220 --> 00:22:54,018
- I can't get the scope in his throat.
- Stop that.

316
00:22:54,089 --> 00:22:56,649
- He's having an allergic reaction. He's crashing.
- No, he's not. Look at his vitals.

317
00:22:56,725 --> 00:22:59,751
O2 sats are within range.

318
00:22:59,828 --> 00:23:02,388
I'm betting the only abnormal sign
is sweaty palms.

319
00:23:04,566 --> 00:23:06,898
Push two milligrams Ativan.

320
00:23:06,969 --> 00:23:09,130
[Mark Gasping]

321
00:23:20,050 --> 00:23:22,848
No allergic reaction.
Just a panic attack.

322
00:23:22,919 --> 00:23:26,855
Something obviously freaked him out.

323
00:23:28,825 --> 00:23:30,759
Can we talk now?

324
00:23:33,564 --> 00:23:35,498
You couldn'tjust come
into the room?

325
00:23:35,566 --> 00:23:39,332
He's had five visitors drive down.
I didn't recognize any of them.

326
00:23:39,403 --> 00:23:42,566
Six more have sent him flowers,
candy and a teddy bear,

327
00:23:42,640 --> 00:23:45,074
which I'm sure he finds
very comforting.

328
00:23:45,142 --> 00:23:47,610
But I didn't recognize any
of the names on the cards.

329
00:23:47,678 --> 00:23:50,044
Shockingly, Mark has friends,
and I have some new ones.

330
00:23:50,114 --> 00:23:53,208
No, it's not shocking
that you have new friends,

331
00:23:53,285 --> 00:23:56,186
but it is shocking that you apparently
dumped all your old ones.

332
00:23:56,254 --> 00:24:01,055
- I haven't.
- No, I didn't think so.

333
00:24:01,126 --> 00:24:04,425
I just think you didn't tell any of them
that you were down here.

334
00:24:04,496 --> 00:24:06,726
Now, why would that be?

335
00:24:08,667 --> 00:24:11,500
What are you up to?
It's gonna take a little while.

336
00:24:12,504 --> 00:24:15,769
Why would you not tell
your oldest friends...

337
00:24:15,842 --> 00:24:20,302
that you were taking Mark
to Princeton Plainsboro Teaching
Hospital to try to save his life?

338
00:24:20,380 --> 00:24:22,712
I've been busy. I haven't kept track
of who knows what.

339
00:24:22,782 --> 00:24:26,878
See, my old friends
are telling me to be careful.
They seem to think-

340
00:24:26,953 --> 00:24:29,114
He.
And he sent me the bear.

341
00:24:29,188 --> 00:24:31,679
Figures.

342
00:24:31,758 --> 00:24:35,990
He seems to think
that I'm not over you...

343
00:24:37,197 --> 00:24:40,963
and that it might be dangerous for me
to spend time with you.

344
00:24:41,034 --> 00:24:45,471
I'm thinking your friends
might have similar concerns,

345
00:24:45,539 --> 00:24:47,632
and so you didn't tell them
you'd be here with me.

346
00:24:47,708 --> 00:24:50,199
What's your point?
That I'm still in love with you?

347
00:24:50,277 --> 00:24:53,269
I should abandon my dying husband,
and we should head for Rio.

348
00:24:55,716 --> 00:24:57,741
No.

349
00:24:57,819 --> 00:25:01,949
Greg, I appreciate
what you're doing for us,
but I think maybe Wilson's right.

350
00:25:02,023 --> 00:25:05,754
Maybe you should
just stay away from me.

351
00:25:14,135 --> 00:25:16,695
[Fax Machine Rings]

352
00:25:32,888 --> 00:25:35,823
~ [Background]

353
00:25:45,535 --> 00:25:47,696
What's up?

354
00:25:47,771 --> 00:25:51,036
[Taps Glass On Bar]
Love the bear.
It's adorable.

355
00:25:51,107 --> 00:25:54,508
My wife's gonna kill me.
We're having company.
She cooked.

356
00:25:54,578 --> 00:25:56,910
I got Mark's latest blood work.

357
00:25:56,980 --> 00:25:59,847
He's not responding
to treatment.

358
00:26:01,485 --> 00:26:03,419
I'm sorry.

359
00:26:03,488 --> 00:26:06,048
I was happy.

360
00:26:10,161 --> 00:26:12,823
He's my patient.

361
00:26:12,897 --> 00:26:16,060
I'm sure he's a good guy.
He's probably a great guy.

362
00:26:16,133 --> 00:26:18,533
He's apparently
a much better guy than I am.

363
00:26:20,137 --> 00:26:23,004
And some part of me
wants him to die.

364
00:26:28,413 --> 00:26:32,907
I'm just not sure if it's because...
I want to be with her,

365
00:26:32,985 --> 00:26:36,648
or it's because
I want her to suffer.

366
00:26:49,168 --> 00:26:52,865
Hey. Is it okay if I talk
to Stacy for a minute?

367
00:26:54,607 --> 00:26:57,098
I'll be just outside, honey.

368
00:27:04,884 --> 00:27:07,079
You two are good together.

369
00:27:07,153 --> 00:27:09,451
You know nothing about Mark.

370
00:27:09,523 --> 00:27:11,582
He took you to Paris.

371
00:27:11,659 --> 00:27:13,650
That's good enough for me.

372
00:27:16,664 --> 00:27:18,723
We never went to Paris.

373
00:27:20,501 --> 00:27:23,732
Your honeymoon.
It's been your dream city.

374
00:27:23,804 --> 00:27:27,740
You wanted to go since you were 16.
He actually took you.
No, I had to work.

375
00:27:27,808 --> 00:27:32,677
We spent the night in New York.
Then we went back to Short Hills.
What is it?

376
00:27:32,747 --> 00:27:36,843
When did Mark switch
from mountain biking to yoga?

377
00:27:36,918 --> 00:27:39,079
About a month ago.

378
00:27:39,154 --> 00:27:41,645
The same time
he started getting sick.
What does that mean?

379
00:27:41,723 --> 00:27:44,214
We have two more symptoms.

380
00:27:46,428 --> 00:27:50,762
The patient was asked
a series of questions to determine
the functionality of his brain.

381
00:27:50,832 --> 00:27:54,529
- You grilled him about Stacy.
- Whatever.

382
00:27:54,604 --> 00:27:58,301
Yeah, the point is, he told us everything
we need to know to diagnose him.

383
00:27:58,374 --> 00:28:01,434
That is, if we use your fancy PET scan
as a lie detector.

384
00:28:01,510 --> 00:28:03,501
See, it's a very creative process,
lying.

385
00:28:03,579 --> 00:28:07,948
Now, telling the truth
is a much simpler process.
See here.

386
00:28:08,017 --> 00:28:12,818
Question nine. This is where Mark
gives a long, rambling answer
about taking Stacy to Paris.

387
00:28:12,888 --> 00:28:17,120
- What does the PET scan say?
- Minimal involvement.
Just the frontal and temporal lobes.

388
00:28:17,194 --> 00:28:20,357
He said he went to Paris,
and the PET confirms it.
So, what?

389
00:28:20,430 --> 00:28:23,228
They didn't go.

390
00:28:23,300 --> 00:28:26,235
They didn't go to Paris,
and yet Mark's brain
apparently thinks...

391
00:28:26,303 --> 00:28:30,034
that he really did spend 40 francs
on a tour of the bastille.

392
00:28:30,107 --> 00:28:34,203
So we have an intermittent syndrome
that presents with abdominal pain,

393
00:28:34,277 --> 00:28:37,075
polyneuropathy, paranoia...

394
00:28:37,148 --> 00:28:40,549
and delusions.

395
00:28:40,618 --> 00:28:44,145
Now, here's the thing
about Acute Intermittent Porphyria.

396
00:28:44,222 --> 00:28:47,680
It'll jump you in a dark alley,
beat the crap out of you,
leave you bleeding.

397
00:28:47,759 --> 00:28:50,455
But it wears gloves,
so no fingerprints.

398
00:28:50,528 --> 00:28:54,225
Doesn't show up in blood tests,
urine tests, nothing...

399
00:28:54,298 --> 00:28:57,529
unless you catch it red-handed
in the middle of an attack.

400
00:28:57,603 --> 00:29:00,766
But there are other
symptoms of A.I.P.

401
00:29:00,839 --> 00:29:03,273
- Such as?
- Light sensitivity.

402
00:29:03,342 --> 00:29:06,334
Yeah. One of the true tragedies
of this condition...

403
00:29:06,412 --> 00:29:09,575
is it makes you want to stick
your cool new mountain bike
in the garage...

404
00:29:09,648 --> 00:29:12,048
and take up an indoor sport
like, say, yoga.

405
00:29:12,117 --> 00:29:15,052
Start the treatment.
Hematin and glucose.

406
00:29:15,120 --> 00:29:18,351
If you give him Hematin now
and you're wrong, he dies today.

407
00:29:18,424 --> 00:29:22,019
There's only one way to confirm A.I.P. -
urine sample made during the attack.

408
00:29:22,095 --> 00:29:24,723
And there's no way to predict
when he'll have another attack.

409
00:29:24,798 --> 00:29:27,289
Sure there is.

410
00:29:27,367 --> 00:29:30,803
Acute Intermittent Porphyria
has very specific triggers.

411
00:29:30,870 --> 00:29:34,966
Barbiturates, alcohol,
high levels of protein
will set off an attack.

412
00:29:35,041 --> 00:29:38,636
Which trigger
do you think set off Mark's?
Not the faintest idea.

413
00:29:38,712 --> 00:29:41,943
That's why I'm gonna give him
the combo plate. All of it.

414
00:29:42,016 --> 00:29:45,816
So ifhe has this,
and you trigger an attack,
the attack makes him worse, right?

415
00:29:45,886 --> 00:29:49,287
Yeah. But then we'll know what it is
and we can treat it.

416
00:29:51,759 --> 00:29:57,493
But if I don't have this thing
and you give me that shot,
what happens?

417
00:29:59,033 --> 00:30:03,129
No idea. If we don't know
what's messing up your brain,
we don't know how you'll react.

418
00:30:07,843 --> 00:30:10,937
Okay, l- I need a minute
with my husband.

419
00:30:26,496 --> 00:30:29,431
[No Audible Dialogue]

420
00:30:44,447 --> 00:30:47,314
He doesn't want the trigger.

421
00:30:47,384 --> 00:30:50,979
He wants to wait,
see if we can come up
with another explanation.

422
00:30:52,122 --> 00:30:54,784
If it is A.I.P.,
how much time does he have?

423
00:30:54,858 --> 00:30:58,692
No idea.
Next attack could be fatal.

424
00:30:58,762 --> 00:31:01,390
Could be six months from now.
Could be five minutes from now.

425
00:31:02,933 --> 00:31:05,231
I want you to test him.
Fine.

426
00:31:05,302 --> 00:31:07,793
I'll send for
an H.M.B. -synthase mutation.

427
00:31:07,872 --> 00:31:10,670
Genetic test.
Lab will get back to us in a month.

428
00:31:10,742 --> 00:31:13,302
Give him the cocktail,
set off an attack.

429
00:31:13,378 --> 00:31:15,903
No.
Why not?

430
00:31:15,980 --> 00:31:18,073
Because he doesn't want me to.

431
00:31:18,149 --> 00:31:20,640
I'm not gonna sue you.
I'm not gonna report you.

432
00:31:20,718 --> 00:31:22,652
He might.
He's paralyzed.

433
00:31:22,720 --> 00:31:25,484
Either you cure him,
or he won't be writing any letters.

434
00:31:25,557 --> 00:31:27,718
I'm not gonna do it.
Why not?

435
00:31:27,792 --> 00:31:30,158
You keep asking me that question.
My answer doesn't change.

436
00:31:30,229 --> 00:31:33,198
I gave him the parameters.
It's his call.

437
00:31:36,135 --> 00:31:39,195
You want him to die.
I diagnosed him.
I did my job.

438
00:31:39,271 --> 00:31:44,072
You want somebody to tie him down
and force him into treatment,
you're way better at that than I am.

439
00:31:44,143 --> 00:31:48,409
Is that what this is? Payback for your leg?
How many times have we been over this?
I saved your life.

440
00:31:48,480 --> 00:31:52,712
Yeah, maybe.
You're gonna kill my husband
to teach me a lesson?

441
00:31:52,786 --> 00:31:55,380
No. He's gonna die
'cause he's too stubborn
to make the right choice.

442
00:31:55,455 --> 00:31:57,616
Now we're in your territory.

443
00:31:59,359 --> 00:32:02,487
I'm respecting your husband's decision.
I don't see why you've got
a problem with that.

444
00:32:02,562 --> 00:32:04,530
Because it's crap.

445
00:32:04,598 --> 00:32:07,533
Because you browbeat patients,
intimidate them, lie to them.

446
00:32:07,601 --> 00:32:10,593
If you think you're right,
you don't give a damn what they think.

447
00:32:10,670 --> 00:32:13,901
I did what you do all the time.
The only difference is, I did it to you.

448
00:32:19,614 --> 00:32:21,809
He'll never forgive you.

449
00:32:23,117 --> 00:32:25,779
Yeah. He will.

450
00:32:39,234 --> 00:32:41,725
Still no change.
He's not getting worse?

451
00:32:41,803 --> 00:32:45,330
- No. No change at all.
- And that's consistent with A.I.P., right?

452
00:32:45,407 --> 00:32:48,740
Until he has another attack,
his condition's stable.
Yeah.

453
00:32:48,810 --> 00:32:53,440
- Mark, you've got to.
- I don't want to take that test,
not until they're sure.

454
00:32:53,515 --> 00:32:56,006
- You don't know Greg.
- Not like you do.

455
00:32:57,320 --> 00:32:59,185
I only met him
when he drugged me.

456
00:32:59,255 --> 00:33:01,849
Boy, are my ears burning.

457
00:33:01,925 --> 00:33:04,951
- What's that?
- Cocktail hour.

458
00:33:05,028 --> 00:33:07,963
Just 'cause you can't hoist a few
doesn't mean you should be left out.

459
00:33:08,031 --> 00:33:11,797
- Get away from me.
- Mark, if this is what he thinks
is wrong with you-

460
00:33:11,868 --> 00:33:14,132
You trust his judgment
more than mine?
His medical judgment.

461
00:33:14,203 --> 00:33:16,262
And you'd bet my life on that?
I would.

462
00:33:16,339 --> 00:33:18,034
I don't.

463
00:33:18,107 --> 00:33:20,769
Smart.

464
00:33:20,844 --> 00:33:23,335
It's too bad you're paralyzed.

465
00:33:31,889 --> 00:33:34,551
Bing!
Paging Dr. Foreman.

466
00:33:35,426 --> 00:33:39,328
Leave the room.
It's not your problem.
You need the consent from him.

467
00:33:39,396 --> 00:33:42,729
But, Doc,
he ain't right in the head.
Then you need a court order.

468
00:33:42,801 --> 00:33:46,737
Okay. Then get one.
We'll wait here. I won't do nothin'.

469
00:33:50,542 --> 00:33:53,534
Oh! Love the Musketeer thing.

470
00:33:53,611 --> 00:33:56,580
- I got goose bumps.
- Give me the syringe.

471
00:33:56,648 --> 00:34:01,483
Please. If you're right,
this may be his only shot.

472
00:34:01,553 --> 00:34:05,216
So what's your plan?
You take the big dark one,
I've got the little girl,

473
00:34:05,291 --> 00:34:08,089
and the Aussie
will run like a scared wombat
if things turn rough.

474
00:34:15,735 --> 00:34:18,203
I can't do it.

475
00:34:25,912 --> 00:34:27,777
[Gasps]

476
00:34:27,848 --> 00:34:30,408
You son of a bitch!

477
00:34:30,484 --> 00:34:32,679
See what I did there?

478
00:34:38,425 --> 00:34:40,859
- When does it happen?
- If he had A.I.P.,
should've already happened.

479
00:34:40,927 --> 00:34:43,293
- Everyone's different.
- [Foreman] This is not good.

480
00:34:43,363 --> 00:34:46,526
He could have an embolism,
tachycardia, stroke.

481
00:34:46,601 --> 00:34:48,592
What's happening?
Two milligrams of Ativan.

482
00:34:48,669 --> 00:34:51,035
- Is that an attack?
- [Grunts]

483
00:34:52,040 --> 00:34:55,407
- Here.
- No. You'll pollute the sample.

484
00:34:55,476 --> 00:34:59,071
- Chase, get urine from the catheter.
- It's not an attack! He's stroking.

485
00:34:59,147 --> 00:35:01,047
- [Screaming]
- He needs Ativan!

486
00:35:01,115 --> 00:35:04,812
This is not a stroke.
The delta-wave burst
is just a vasospasm.

487
00:35:04,886 --> 00:35:08,754
- Catheter's out.
There's no way to collect the sample.
- Heart rate's in the 40s. Bradycardia.

488
00:35:08,824 --> 00:35:11,315
- We're losing him.
- Hold him down.
- Give him something!

489
00:35:11,393 --> 00:35:13,953
No painkillers.
You were wrong.

490
00:35:16,298 --> 00:35:21,099
[Mark Screaming]

491
00:35:23,472 --> 00:35:26,771
Straight from the bladder.
That's as fresh as it gets.

492
00:35:26,842 --> 00:35:29,675
- [Screaming]
- Would you give him the Ativan already?

493
00:35:29,745 --> 00:35:33,272
He doesn't need
to be awake for this.

494
00:35:55,272 --> 00:35:58,139
It's still yellow.

495
00:35:58,209 --> 00:36:00,370
Move.

496
00:36:00,444 --> 00:36:04,540
- What?
- You think another light's
gonna make the difference?

497
00:36:04,615 --> 00:36:08,278
Organic chem-
more light, more oxidation.

498
00:36:08,352 --> 00:36:10,343
Does that ring any bells?

499
00:36:19,631 --> 00:36:24,091
Start the patient
on 150 milligrams glucose,
75 milligrams Hematin.

500
00:36:39,552 --> 00:36:43,215
[Thunder Rumbling]

501
00:36:51,797 --> 00:36:54,789
Hey.
Hey.

502
00:36:54,867 --> 00:36:57,893
You wanna thumb wrestle?

503
00:36:57,971 --> 00:36:59,962
Come on.
Oh!

504
00:37:03,844 --> 00:37:06,176
He's still a maniac.
I know.

505
00:37:30,338 --> 00:37:32,135
Dr. House?

506
00:37:32,207 --> 00:37:34,198
How's he doing?

507
00:37:37,045 --> 00:37:38,979
Never better.

508
00:37:43,886 --> 00:37:45,877
I thought...

509
00:37:45,955 --> 00:37:49,721
you were too screwed up
to love anyone.

510
00:37:49,792 --> 00:37:51,783
I was wrong.

511
00:37:53,495 --> 00:37:56,726
You just couldn't love me.

512
00:37:59,401 --> 00:38:01,392
That's good.

513
00:38:03,240 --> 00:38:05,231
I'm happy for you.

514
00:38:25,663 --> 00:38:28,689
~ [Stereo: Blues]

515
00:38:48,053 --> 00:38:49,987
~ [Off]

516
00:38:50,055 --> 00:38:52,615
You fixed him.

517
00:38:54,526 --> 00:38:56,721
De nada.

518
00:38:56,795 --> 00:38:58,729
Thank you.

519
00:39:00,432 --> 00:39:02,457
You were right.

520
00:39:02,534 --> 00:39:04,365
He's gonna be fine.

521
00:39:04,436 --> 00:39:06,427
No. About me.

522
00:39:08,308 --> 00:39:10,242
I'm not over you.

523
00:39:13,646 --> 00:39:17,173
You were-You were the one.

524
00:39:17,250 --> 00:39:19,184
You always will be.

525
00:39:23,222 --> 00:39:25,213
But I can't be with you.

526
00:39:34,401 --> 00:39:36,392
So I'm the guy,

527
00:39:37,471 --> 00:39:39,462
but you want the other guy,

528
00:39:39,540 --> 00:39:42,668
who, by definition,
can never be the guy.

529
00:39:45,479 --> 00:39:47,879
What's so great about you
is you always think you're right.

530
00:39:47,948 --> 00:39:52,044
And what's so frustrating
about you is you are right
so much of the time.

531
00:39:55,090 --> 00:39:57,024
You are brilliant,

532
00:39:57,092 --> 00:40:00,425
funny, surprising, sexy.

533
00:40:05,767 --> 00:40:10,466
But with you, I was lonely.
And with Mark, there's room for me.

534
00:40:14,543 --> 00:40:16,909
Okay.

535
00:41:02,226 --> 00:41:04,820
I want to run something by you.

536
00:41:04,896 --> 00:41:07,763
I will not have sex with you!
Not again.

537
00:41:07,832 --> 00:41:10,357
It was miserable
that first time.

538
00:41:10,435 --> 00:41:12,528
All that desperate
administrative need.

539
00:41:12,603 --> 00:41:17,006
Stacy's husband is going to need
close monitoring at the hospital,

540
00:41:17,074 --> 00:41:19,565
and since we can definitely
use her back here,

541
00:41:19,645 --> 00:41:22,808
I've offered her a job-
general counsel.

542
00:41:23,949 --> 00:41:25,883
Did she say yes?

543
00:41:25,951 --> 00:41:29,978
She said only if
it was okay with you.

544
00:41:32,090 --> 00:41:34,024
Yes or no?

545
00:41:41,000 --> 00:41:43,161
Fine. Good.

546
00:44:04,416 --> 00:44:05,678
That's some bad hat, Harry.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
